problems.solution:
Paste this into the editor and press Run.

```ts
const { browser, page } = await launch();
await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

await page.getByPlaceholder("First Name").fill("Grace");
await page.getByPlaceholder("Last Name").fill("Hopper");
await page.locator("textarea").fill("1 Compiler Lane");
await page.locator("input[type='email']").fill("grace.hopper@example.com");
await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill("5559876543");

await show(page);
await browser.close();
```

**Why each locator is the one it is.** First Name and Last Name both carry unique placeholder text, so `getByPlaceholder` reads best. Address has **no** placeholder and no label — but it is the only `<textarea>` on the page, so `page.locator("textarea")` is unambiguous. Email and Phone are reached by input type: `input[type='email']` and `input[type='tel']` each match exactly one control here.

**Check it the way the problem asks:** the screenshot shows all five filled, and no line threw. If one had matched nothing, Playwright would have waited the full timeout and then failed with a clear "waiting for locator" — not silently skipped it.

> `.fill()` and not `.type()` — Day 1.2 measured 68 ms against 92 ms on the same string. Reach for `.pressSequentially()` only when the page reacts to individual keystrokes, such as an autocomplete.

