problems.solution:
```ts
const { browser, page } = await launch();
await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

// The label on this page really is spelled "FeMale" - a gotcha, not a typo to fix.
await page.locator("input[value='FeMale']").check();

await page.locator("#checkbox3").check(); // Hockey
await page.locator("#checkbox1").check(); // Cricket - a second hobby, allowed

// #Skills looks like a multi-select (78 options) but has no `multiple` attribute,
// so it keeps only the LAST pick. Two calls, one survivor.
await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption({ label: "Java" });
await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption({ label: "Python" });

// Two selects mention Country. #countries ("Country*") is a dead field with only a
// placeholder option; the live one is #country, labelled "Select Country :".
await page.locator("#country").selectOption("India");

await show(page);
await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
await browser.close();
```

**The two traps this problem is built around.**

**"Two skills" is not achievable on this control.** `#Skills` has no `multiple` attribute, so the second `selectOption` replaces the first rather than adding to it — the screenshot will show Python alone. That is the page being honest about what it is. Recognising it beats writing a passing test that claims two skills were chosen.

**`FeMale`.** Searching the DOM for `Female` returns nothing, and it is tempting to assume the radio is broken. Read the attribute before you trust your memory of it.

> Take the screenshot **before** the click. After submit the page changes, and you lose the evidence of what you actually filled in.

