problems.solution:
Two edits. First `week2/generated/registration-helpers.ts` — add a second parameter rather than a second function:

```ts
import type { Page } from "@playwright/test";

export async function fillRegistrationForm(page: Page, email: string, phone: string) {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  await page.getByPlaceholder("First Name").fill("Ada");
  await page.getByPlaceholder("Last Name").fill("Lovelace");
  await page.locator("textarea").fill("123 Analytical Engine Way");
  await page.locator("input[type='email']").fill(email);
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phone);
}
```

Then a spec that calls it twice:

```ts
import { test } from "@playwright/test";
import { fillRegistrationForm } from "./registration-helpers";

test("registers learner one", async ({ page }) => {
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.one@example.com", "5550000001");
  await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
});

test("registers learner two", async ({ page }) => {
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.two@example.com", "5550000002");
  await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
});
```

```bash
npx playwright test week2/generated/day1-practice-reuse.spec.ts
```
→ `2 passed`.

**The judgement being trained here.** The obvious alternative is a second function, `fillRegistrationFormWithPhone`. Resist it: two functions that differ by one line drift apart the first time the form changes, and then you have two truths about the same page. A parameter keeps one.

The things that *stay* hard-coded — the name, the address — stay that way deliberately. Parameterise what a caller actually varies; parameterising everything just reinvents `page.fill` with extra steps.

> The helper file is `registration-helpers.ts`, **not** `.spec.ts`. Give it the spec suffix and the runner will collect a file containing no tests.
